WEBVTT

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:07.560
It's important to realise that the gliders are designed

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:12.280
according to the rules and the scoring the rule have a very

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:16.200
high impact on the way we fly as a competitor and the way we

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:21.840
design as a designer. If the legislators decide that

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:26.120
any competition should be raised on ENC wings, then the

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:29.560
manufacturer will produce very specific ENC wings, and it

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.840
doesn't mean that someone which was happily flying an ENC wing

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.960
before will be able to fly this ENC competition wings.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:42.760
Do you guys still discuss making Enzo 3 better or is this more or

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.000
less like Enzo 4 is only possible if the sport sees a

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.840
paradigm shift in its paperwork 1st and then the designers will

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:51.360
get to. Work.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.360
We're trying to do an Enzo for whatever are the rules.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.560
We are working since maybe five years and we're trying to make

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.040
progress every glider, otherwise we don't release.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:05.640
Hello and welcome back to Paragliding After Us.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:09.760
I'm your host, Aninda Singh. And joining us today is the man

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.040
himself, Luke Armand. Luke, this has been a long time

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.280
coming. Welcome to the show.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.560
Thank you Luke. I want to dive right in and, you

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:26.920
know, compare let's say 2011's situation to 2025.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.360
The industry has seen fair bit of evolution over the past

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.200
years, but a debate or a safety debate, as Gavin called in his

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:41.920
show has raged on once again. And back in 2011, we had sports

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.560
class, which was deemed to be too dangerous or open class,

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:46.800
which was deemed to be too dangerous.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.120
And people said that we need a reform of some sorts.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.520
Fast forward to 2025. We have put in all the, you

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.000
know, time, energy and money in making wings safer and more fun.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:01.440
And yet we are standing at the same question once again.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:05.320
So from your eyes, who has seen the industry evolve?

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.039
Is this something different, or is this just history repeating

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:16.480
itself all over again? No, I don't think the history

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.160
repeats. Things are evolving, but we keep

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:26.080
having accidents but we try to progress.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:32.880
I mean everyone, manufacturers, but also obviously organisers.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.880
By those everything, I mean we're doing fantastic sport

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.080
there. We're having a lot of fun.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:43.200
It's it's really great. It's a great, great sport.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:48.360
But unfortunately sometimes there is some accidents and so

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.920
we can only try to get things better.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.360
Fair enough. Luke, if we have to look at the

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:02.560
main reasons why open class had to go away, is it something

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.200
similar to the reasons that are being called out for the too

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.840
much focus on performance versus too little focus on safety?

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.200
Because I think Kriegel came up with a statement a few days ago

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:19.320
said that we tend to focus on one aspect when that one is

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.720
weighing heavy and the other aspect takes a back seat.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:26.400
But then when we reach a period where we don't see that many,

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.360
you know, mishaps or something and then we just go back to

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.920
chasing safety. So what's your point of view on

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:39.280
this? There are several things on your

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:45.080
question, but it's true that the community is reacting to events.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.680
It's very difficult to have the community anticipating things.

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:52.200
So it's when there are some accidents, there are some

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.640
reactions. If there is no accident and some

00:03:55.640 --> 00:04:02.080
people warn for whatever possible problems, usually there

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.000
is no reaction until a problem comes.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:12.320
That's the way things are. And 2011 there has there was a

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:15.760
spectacular problem because two fatality in the first task of

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.720
the words and from there there was a reaction and and move.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.200
And then we also look at something to play with accident

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:30.840
because we only kind of count fatal accidents for some

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:36.120
reasons. And well, the sport is accepted

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:42.120
a lot more broken bones of course than fatal accidents.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:48.160
But there has been a long period without too much fatal

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.720
accidents. And we were community was happy.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:58.880
But at the last words there was 1 fatal accident and all the

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.640
fatal accident of the competitions.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:06.960
And so there is a reaction which is which is a fair reaction to

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:12.000
try to improve things. So there was also a question

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:18.280
about open class. I can clarify that, you know.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.680
Yeah, yeah. But I can answer, I think open

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:26.280
class was obviously missing a little bit of testing from some

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:33.960
manufacturers and there was some sometimes some not so good

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:42.240
behaviour in some gliders that was responsible for really some

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.640
accident pilot couldn't do anything about.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:49.040
But there was also other things that has been corrected.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:53.400
It's not only the gliders. At that time final glide was

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:57.840
very dangerous. It's important to realise that

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:04.280
gliders are designed according to the rules and the scoring.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:10.080
The scoring is very important and at that time you were not

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.960
having so much leading points, mostly time points.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:19.160
And you, you could every minute, if I remember well, you could

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:26.080
gain 30 time points. On top of that, the competition

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:30.240
with the two fatalities on the first task was happening in

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:34.640
which is very lifty era with convergence and the final glide

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:38.640
was in convergence. So we're talking of final glide

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:43.720
with excess altitude and final glide with excess altitude.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:50.440
If you just go to a cylinder, there is no brain to have.

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:52.600
You just need to go as fast as possible.

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:55.120
There is no Mac ready anymore, nothing.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:07:03.440
You just go as fast as possible and you earn maybe 25 or 30

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:09.960
points per minute when one point can make a difference in the

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:15.760
overall of the competition. So the format make was making a

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:19.200
really big push for the glider to have absolute top speed

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:25.000
without so much testing dreamers on the risers.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.680
So a lot of ingredients to create accidents.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.440
Fair enough. I think that is a very common

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:34.360
analogy which is used in aviation in general, that it

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:39.320
does not take one mess up which contributes to the mishap

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:40.800
eventually. It's a it's a combination of

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.320
things which lead us there. But I think you touched upon a

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:47.600
very important point there where you mentioned that how we fly

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:52.320
depends a lot about legislation and the scoring and how we push

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.800
the limits. So more or less, if I have to

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:00.080
narrow this down for easier understanding of our audiences,

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:06.360
are we not having enough learned people in the sport who want to

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:11.000
sit down and do the boring part? Because regardless, anybody who

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:14.520
gets pushed into the sport is coming in for the joy of flight

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:21.120
and legislation, paperwork and all the, you know, bookkeeping

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:26.560
is usually done by people who are not so or in a professional

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:31.160
manner who are not so much into outdoor extreme adventure sports

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:34.000
or something. Whereas our sport is mostly

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:37.360
consisting of pilots who are picking up various roles.

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:43.919
So do you see a lack of skilled brainpower sitting down and

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:45.600
saying that I want to work towards this?

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:50.160
Or is this just evolution taking its own course, more or less?

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:55.720
I don't think so. I think there, there is a lot of

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:01.360
good brains working on the rules and scoring and stuff, but it

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:04.040
needs some drive. I mean a lot of these guy, they

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:06.200
are also pilots, passionated pilots.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:12.720
I also tried to spend a bit of my working time on on the

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:20.920
subject, participate some expert group and give some technical

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:25.120
inputs. But it's also difficult to get

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:31.040
things working efficiently in in some groups because you need to

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:36.160
agree it's good to have a lot of brain working together, but it

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:40.240
needs also some efficiency in the debate and some some

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:42.600
leadership. And it's difficult to organise

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:47.760
things. But CCC has been created, the

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:51.840
scoring has evolved, the things are evolving in a good manner.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:56.920
It takes time, but these sort of things I think can only take

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:57.640
time. OK.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:59.600
I want to take the example of Ozone.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:03.560
We have seen a lot of brands come and go in the industry.

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:09.120
Legacy brands are few, but the ones who exist are more or less

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:11.600
cementing their place as the time goes on.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:18.800
Mostly brands have been started, as you said that you know, we

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:20.040
are a bunch of passionate pilots.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:22.720
So somebody is like, OK, I want to start make a living out of

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:24.520
this. I'll go out and put my own brand

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:27.080
and invest in R&D and collect the team.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:33.240
So Ozone has done a lot of things right to come as one of

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:36.720
the top contenders when it comes to wing choice.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:41.520
And I believe Ozone needed a lot of paperwork, a lot of R&D and a

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:43.960
lot of other things that contribute to the success

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:49.200
eventually. So if FAI or civil or any of

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:53.120
these, you know, the the companies or maybe even European

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:58.720
norms if they were run commercially like the brand, is

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:03.320
there more chances of success? Because I think money is a big

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:06.840
problem that anybody who's coming in is doing it more or

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:09.520
less out of passion and not out of profit.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:19.280
Yes, but you know, like the World Cup, for example, is, is

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:22.280
a, is a kind of company, it's a non profit company, but it needs

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:29.960
to to not lose money. So the organisers, all the

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:31.960
organisers, they need to organise things.

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:34.280
They do that not for earning money.

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:37.400
Most of them is for the passion as well.

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:44.080
Uh but they need to organise the things efficiently so they don't

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:47.520
lose money. Don't know if that's answering

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:49.760
your question, maybe I haven't understood it.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:51.560
Correctly. It's, it's, it's basically like,

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:55.720
are we not having professional structures in place enough that

00:11:55.960 --> 00:12:00.040
invite the right people? More or less because Ozone had a

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:03.080
professional structure based on, let's say, the model of

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:07.480
capitalism and they succeeded in surviving where other brands

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:12.520
eventually lost out on the race. So if Ozone can do it when it

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:17.240
comes to commercializing the, the, the selling part, then why

00:12:17.240 --> 00:12:20.560
can't we professionalize the structure when it comes to

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:23.560
managing the sport? That is my dilemma here.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:28.040
OK. I think it's quite professional

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:31.320
in a way that the organiser are in competition.

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:37.480
I mean if you speak about capitalism, organisers are in

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:42.400
competition in a way. So they they are pushed to do a

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:45.640
good job. I don't blame organiser or Cava

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:49.480
or stuff like that. They everything can be more

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:54.440
efficient and but there are some feedbacks, there are some

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:58.600
competition. There was a time where PWVC was

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:03.120
in competition with the area and they were pushing each other.

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:08.720
At the moment it's kind of the same leading team, which also

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:13.200
gives some advantages because everything can be equal in the

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:18.720
scoring and. Let's talk about the legislation

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:21.480
a bit. You know, I think as you

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:28.320
mentioned that it the way sport evolves is a lot about how the

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:34.200
rules are set. Rules do change, but not up to a

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:39.000
large extent. Are we, are we lacking

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:43.720
tightening of rules at a lower level which eventually leads to

00:13:43.720 --> 00:13:47.080
this kind of an attitude once we reach the professional levels or

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:52.840
what is kind of lacking that allows us to make machines which

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:56.880
are really fun, really cool to fly, but when they go bad, they

00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:59.680
go really bad. And sometimes the the cost that

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:01.920
we pay is is the highest possible one.

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:05.640
About holes, you you talking about Section 7?

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:09.760
Not just that, also the guidelines that manufacturers

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:14.320
have to adhere to, because, regardless, it is the European

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:17.800
norms based on which the equipment is being produced.

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:21.000
Section 7, of course, has its own importance when it comes to

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:26.400
racing, but more or less the. Ecosystem of rules, yeah.

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:31.240
So there are many things behind rules here you have European

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:38.400
norm which is driven by European administration with a group of

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:42.320
experts trying to make it evolve and it takes time.

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:54.040
There is the Section 7 which includes the the CCC rules and

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:57.800
all the scoring things. This is a big book of rules for

00:14:57.800 --> 00:15:06.320
competition driven by CIBA and there is a PWVC rules which also

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:09.640
is in accordance with Section 7 anyway mostly.

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:17.040
But all these rules have to be manipulate with a lot of

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:22.280
cautious because it's very difficult to anticipate the

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:25.600
consequences of any change of these rules.

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:33.040
And so often the people writing these rules, they want to try

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:39.720
test before really applying them because they may have some good

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:42.440
effect and they may have some side effect that you don't know

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:46.880
and it's difficult to anticipate.

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:52.440
So there is a lot of debate of experts and some testing before

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:55.280
a rule is is set a a change is set.

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:58.120
So you mean prototyping of some sorts?

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:00.640
Basically when you guys come out with a new product, you

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:05.160
prototype it and then you know, do a hit and try method.

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:09.320
And that is kind of lacking over here, that we don't have a

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:12.520
foolproof method where we can test the success of rules and

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:18.640
the only way is to go out and keep on going ahead with the

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:22.320
challenges that we face like it. It just seems a very lost case.

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:27.200
And and in that regard that we don't know which rule will work,

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:28.680
but we can only keep on trying. Yeah.

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:33.000
Yeah, I mean, the, the, the rules are tested and they

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:38.280
eventually are changed, you know, yeah, norms is changing

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:42.440
like every two or three years. Section 7 is changing every

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:45.520
year. A lot of details like way of

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:53.200
calculation in the end of speed in in leading points.

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:57.560
CCC rules are also evolving. There is a lot of debate now,

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:04.599
but the rule have a very high impact on on the way we fly as a

00:17:04.599 --> 00:17:08.359
competitor and on the way we design as a designer.

00:17:08.359 --> 00:17:11.839
But this effect is very difficult to anticipate.

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:16.680
Let's talk about ENC class. It's not that long ago that the

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:20.000
whole class saw revolution of some sorts.

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:26.960
From your perspective, who has seen the sport go from open to

00:17:26.960 --> 00:17:33.400
triple to triple C down to There was a time when END's were

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:38.240
considered as the go to gliders for a lot of pilots and now

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:40.600
almost nobody's talking about END.

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:46.600
So how did ENC class get its significance and why do we

00:17:46.600 --> 00:17:51.480
suddenly see a whole new level of performance being pushed onto

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:54.760
a lower category of glider that was never meant to be raced?

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:58.960
It was just a cross country machine for pilots who were

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:05.080
looking not to increase the bar too high but still have the fun.

00:18:05.080 --> 00:18:07.760
Yeah. So yeah, a bit of history here.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:15.960
We had open class after Piedra ETA 2011, it was END.

00:18:16.520 --> 00:18:20.960
The reaction was to forbid open class and glad you need to have

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:23.920
an EN. So the manufacturers they

00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:30.320
produced, the first competition was done with existing ENDS and

00:18:30.320 --> 00:18:36.840
then the manufacturers did specific design END for

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:41.840
competition because manufacturers wanted to win

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:48.080
competitions and this specific design were nothing to do with

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:55.040
the previously existing ENDS. And for example, in Ozone, we

00:18:55.040 --> 00:19:07.280
had the Montra Air 04 and we produce 4 competition, a wing

00:19:07.280 --> 00:19:12.120
called Enzo, which was based on our open class, slightly

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:17.200
modified to pass END norm. But we were still selling the

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:24.240
END for leisure pilots, the targeted pilots that we had

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:27.040
doing cross country experience, cross country pilots.

00:19:27.920 --> 00:19:32.200
And at the same time we were selling the competition END

00:19:32.800 --> 00:19:37.880
glider. But this was putting a lot of

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:43.760
confusion in the market because the the people they had the idea

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:49.760
that EN stamp is, is a way to to know what glider is for them.

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:57.320
So they there is a pilot flying E&D wing and he thinks his level

00:19:57.320 --> 00:20:01.120
is for flying E&D wings, whatever the manufacturer says.

